        <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

          <details class="ar-faq-item" open><summary><h3>What is the MOQ for custom windbreakers from China?</h3></summary><p>MOQ varies by factory and model. For OEM production, common industry ranges sit around 300–1,000 pieces per style and color. ODM programs often start lower, around 200–500 pieces. Wholesale stock orders can go as low as 50–200 pieces. These are general ranges; confirm exact minimums with each supplier, since fabric choice and complexity affect them</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How long does windbreaker sampling and production take?</h3></summary><p>Sampling typically takes around 7–15 days after specs and fabrics are confirmed. Bulk production commonly runs 30–45 days, followed by 25–35 days of sea freight to major markets. These are typical industry ranges. Peak season, fabric availability, and order complexity can extend them, so build buffer into your launch calendar</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>Can I get custom windbreakers with my own logo and design?</h3></summary><p>Yes. Both OEM and ODM models support private label. Under OEM you supply the tech pack and we produce to your specification, including your branding, labels, and packaging. Under ODM you adapt an existing pattern and apply your branding. Logo application options include embroidery, screen print, heat transfer, and woven labels</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What fabrics are best for windbreakers?</h3></summary><p>It depends on the end use. Lightweight packable shells often use polyester or nylon taffeta with a DWR finish. Technical windbreakers use laminated or coated fabrics with a membrane for waterproofing and breathability. Soft-shell options use bonded knit or woven fabrics for stretch and comfort. The right choice depends on your target climate, price point, and whether waterproofing or breathability matters more</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How do I verify a windbreaker manufacturer before ordering?</h3></summary><p>Start with documentation: business license, audit reports, and references from existing buyers. Then order a sample and evaluate seam-sealing, stitching, and finishing. Request a video walkthrough of the production floor if an in-person visit is not possible. Finally, place a small trial order before committing to a large volume</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What certifications should a windbreaker factory have?</h3></summary><p>The relevant certifications depend on your market and buyer requirements. Common social compliance audits include BSCI, WRAP, and Sedex/SMETA, while quality management is often covered by ISO 9001. Some buyers also require specific chemical or environmental standards. Confirm which audits your target retailer accepts, and verify the factory's certificates are current</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>What is the difference between OEM and ODM windbreaker manufacturing?</h3></summary><p>OEM means the factory builds to your design and specifications, and you supply the tech pack. ODM means the factory offers existing designs that you adapt and brand. OEM gives more control and differentiation; ODM is faster and requires less design resource. Many buyers start with ODM and move to OEM as their volume and design capability grow</p></details>

          <details class="ar-faq-item"><summary><h3>How much does it cost to manufacture custom windbreakers in China?</h3></summary><p>Cost depends on fabric, construction, trims, order quantity, and finishing. A basic lightweight shell sits at the lower end, while a seam-sealed technical jacket with a membrane and quality zippers sits higher. FOB pricing for a mid-range custom windbreaker commonly falls in an indicative range of $8–25 per piece for orders around 1,000–3,000 units, excluding trim upgrades and testing fees, though this varies widely. Request itemized quotations rather than single lump-sum figures</p></details>

        <h2 id="next-steps">Final Thoughts &amp; Next Steps</h2>

        <p>The right windbreaker manufacturer is the one that matches your order profile, not the one sitting at the top of someone's list. A high-volume brand needs scale and audit depth. A growing DTC label needs flexibility, patient sampling, and a merchandiser who answers email. A wholesaler needs stock availability and a 30-day turnaround</p>

        <p>Use the six-point checklist to screen, the pitfalls table to build your contract and inspection terms, and the OEM/ODM comparison to lock your working model. Then order a sample. One sample round, inspected properly, tells you more about a factory than ten pages of company profile</p>

